c=concept(484,'Constraint counts and uniqueness','Polynomial coefficient counts, independent constraints, and generic versus special cases.',[484,485])
ask(c,'A polynomial of degree at most n has how many scalar coefficients?', ['n+1','n','2n','n−1'], ['The powers run from t⁰ through tⁿ.','This omits the constant term.','There is one coefficient per power, not two.','This omits two terms.'],pages=[484])
ask(c,'Which generic endpoint-data count corresponds to linear, cubic and quintic interpolation?', ['2, 4 and 6 scalar constraints','1, 3 and 5 scalar constraints','2, 3 and 4 scalar constraints','4, 6 and 8 scalar constraints'], ['Their coefficient counts are 2, 4 and 6.','Polynomial degree is one less than coefficient count.','This undercounts cubic and quintic coefficients.','This overcounts each polynomial’s coefficients.'],pages=[484])
ask(c,'Why is matching the number of equations and polynomial coefficients not by itself enough to prove a unique solution?', ['The equations must also be independent and compatible','Every square linear system is automatically invertible','All boundary times must be identical','Uniqueness requires zero endpoint displacement'], ['A square coefficient matrix can be singular; distinct endpoint times and independent constraints matter.','Square matrices can have zero determinant.','Coincident times can create redundancy or contradiction.','Nonzero displacement can have a unique cubic solution.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[484],conflict=(484,'Number of constraints must equal number of coefficients.','Counting is a generic design guide. A unique solution also requires full rank and consistency; redundant or specially compatible constraints need separate analysis.'))
ask(c,'A straight line θ(t)=θ0+vt already has θ(T)=θ0+vT and equal endpoint velocities v. Can it satisfy those four specially compatible endpoint conditions?', ['Yes; cubic is the generic minimum, not mandatory for this special compatible data set','No; a line can never satisfy more than two stated equations','Only if T=0','Only if both positions are zero'], ['All four conditions hold for that line even though only two are independent.','Additional consistent equations can be redundant.','The example works for any positive T.','The absolute position origin does not matter.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[484,485],assumptions=['T>0 and the listed endpoint data are exactly compatible with the same constant slope.'])
issue(484,'constraint-count','Constraint count is stated as an equality without rank, compatibility or special-case qualifications.','Use independent boundary conditions and T>0 for unique cubic/quintic solutions. Test a compatible linear special case separately.')
c=concept(485,'Why a cubic is the generic minimum','Four independent endpoint constraints and polynomial freedom.',[485,487])
ask(c,'For arbitrary two-endpoint position and velocity data with T>0, why is a cubic the generic minimum degree?', ['Four independent scalar conditions need four coefficients','The number of joints must always be four','A cubic has six independent endpoint accelerations','Every quadratic has zero acceleration'], ['The cubic supplies a0,a1,a2,a3 for four generic conditions.','Degree is selected per scalar trajectory, not by joint count.','There are only two endpoint accelerations and they are not separately prescribed here.','A quadratic generally has constant nonzero acceleration.'],pages=[485])
ask(c,'Which polynomial is the stated cubic joint trajectory?', ['a0+a1t+a2t²+a3t³','a0+a1t+a2t² only','a0+a1sin(t)+a2cos(t)','a0+a1t³ with no other free coefficients'], ['It contains all powers from zero through three.','That is at most quadratic.','That is trigonometric, not a cubic polynomial.','Two free coefficients cannot fit four arbitrary independent conditions.'],pages=[485])
c=concept(486,'Via points and segment-local time','Piecewise cubics, unknown via velocities and correct use of local segment duration.')
ask(c,'With via positions θ_i at times t_i, what is the lecture’s piecewise-cubic construction?', ['Use one cubic on each interval [t_i,t_{i+1}]','Use a different cubic at each instant with no interval','Use only one line regardless of how many via points exist','Use the via positions as torques'], ['Each pair of consecutive via points bounds one interpolating segment.','The coefficients define a function over an interval.','A line cannot generally interpolate arbitrary multiple via positions.','The prescribed data are joint positions.'])
ask(c,'Why do specified via-point positions and times alone not usually determine all cubic segments?', ['Endpoint velocities for the segments must also be assigned or determined','Every cubic must have zero displacement','Cubic coefficients never depend on velocity','Via points cannot be used in joint space'], ['A cubic segment needs four boundary conditions, not merely its two endpoint positions.','Nonzero displacement is entirely possible.','The coefficient formulas include endpoint velocities.','Via-point interpolation is the subject of this slide.'])
ask(c,'A segment runs from absolute time 3 s to 7 s. To reuse formulas derived for a start time of zero, which time variable and duration should be used?', ['u=t−3 s and T=4 s','u=t and T=7 s, without shifting boundary data','u=t−7 s and T=3 s','u=3t and T=10 s'], ['The local clock starts at zero and ends at the elapsed duration 7−3.','This would incorrectly impose the segment start at absolute zero.','This starts the local clock at −4 and uses the wrong duration.','Neither scaling nor summed endpoints is the required shift.'],kind='scenario',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['u here is local time in seconds, not the dimensionless normalized time used elsewhere.'])
ask(c,'Two adjacent cubics share the same via position and the same assigned via velocity. What is guaranteed at that join, without further constraints?', ['C1 continuity, but not necessarily C2','C2 continuity automatically','C0 fails whenever the coefficients differ','Both accelerations must be zero'], ['Position and first derivative match; second derivatives can differ.','Acceleration matching is an additional condition.','Different coefficient sets can meet smoothly at a common endpoint.','A matching velocity need not have zero derivative.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[486,492])
c=concept(487,'Cubic derivatives and boundary substitution','First/second derivatives, endpoint equations, dimensions and time-origin assumptions.')
ask(c,'For θ=a0+a1t+a2t²+a3t³, which velocity is correct?', ['a1+2a2t+3a3t²','a0+a1+2a2t+3a3t²','a1+a2t+a3t²','2a2+6a3t'], ['Differentiate each power of t.','The derivative of constant a0 is zero.','The power-rule factors 2 and 3 are missing.','This is acceleration, the second derivative.'],formula=[CUB])
ask(c,'For the same cubic, which acceleration is correct?', ['2a2+6a3t','a2+3a3t','a1+2a2t+3a3t²','6a3t²'], ['Differentiate the velocity once more.','Both terms miss a factor of two.','This is velocity.','The derivative must reduce the time power, not raise it.'],formula=[CUB])
ask(c,'At a segment start t=0, what are a0 and a1?', ['a0=θ0 and a1=v0','a0=v0 and a1=θ0','a0=0 and a1=0 for every trajectory','a0=θf and a1=vf'], ['Setting t=0 leaves a0 in position and a1 in velocity.','This reverses variables with different dimensions.','Only particular boundary data make both zero.','The initial coefficients encode the initial, not final, conditions.'],formula=[CUB],assumptions=['θ0 and v0 denote the position and velocity at the local start time.'])
ask(c,'Which pair is the final-time cubic position and velocity condition?', ['θf=a0+a1T+a2T²+a3T³; vf=a1+2a2T+3a3T²','θf=a0+a1T+a2T²+a3T³; vf=a0+2a2T+3a3T²','θf=a0+a1T²+a2T³+a3T⁴; vf=a1+2a2T+3a3T²','θf=a0+a1T+a2T²+a3T³; vf=2a2+6a3T'], ['Substitute T into the polynomial and its first derivative.','Velocity has a1, not a0, as its constant term.','The position powers are shifted incorrectly.','The second expression is acceleration rather than velocity.'],formula=[CUB])
ask(c,'If θ is in degrees and t in seconds, which units belong to a2 and a3?', ['deg/s² and deg/s³','deg/s and deg/s²','deg and deg','s²/deg and s³/deg'], ['Each coefficient must cancel its multiplying power of seconds to produce degrees.','These units would give the wrong dimensions in the polynomial.','The coefficients of t² and t³ cannot both have unscaled angle units.','These invert the required dimensions.'],formula=[CUB])
c=concept(488,'Residual equations and cubic elimination','Displacement residual versus total displacement and exact elimination of a2/a3.')
RES=fm('R=θf−θ0−v0T; S=vf−v0; a2T²+a3T³=R; 2a2T+3a3T²=S; a3=S/T²−2R/T³',{'R':'position residual after subtracting initial linear motion','S':'velocity difference','θ0,θf':'endpoint positions','v0,vf':'endpoint velocities','T':'duration','a2,a3':'cubic coefficients'},{'R,θ0,θf':'joint-angle unit','S,v0,vf':'joint-angle unit/s','T':'s','a2':'joint-angle unit/s²','a3':'joint-angle unit/s³'},TIME)
ask(c,'The slide defines Δθ=θf−θ0−v0T. When does this equal the plain displacement θf−θ0?', ['When v0=0, since T>0','Whenever vf=0, regardless of v0','Whenever θ0=0, regardless of v0','For every cubic without qualification'], ['The subtracted linear-motion term then vanishes.','Final velocity does not remove the initial-velocity term.','A zero initial position does not remove v0T.','For nonzero v0, this is a residual rather than the plain displacement.'],formula=[RES],assumptions=TIME)
ask(c,'After defining R=θf−θ0−v0T and S=vf−v0, which two equations remain?', ['a2T²+a3T³=R and 2a2T+3a3T²=S','a2T+a3T²=R and 2a2+3a3T=S','a2T²+a3T³=S and 2a2T+3a3T²=R','a2+a3=R and a2−a3=S'], ['Subtract the known a0 and a1 contributions from the endpoint equations.','These powers arise only after division and would require divided right-hand sides.','This exchanges position and velocity residuals.','The time factors and derivative coefficients cannot be discarded.'],formula=[RES])
ask(c,'Divide the residual position equation by T² and the velocity equation by T. Subtract twice the first from the second. What remains?', ['a3T=S/T−2R/T²','a3T=S/T+2R/T²','a2=S/T−2R/T²','3a3T=S/T−R/T²'], ['The 2a2 terms cancel and 3a3T−2a3T leaves a3T.','The second residual term is subtracted, not added.','The cancellation eliminates a2, not a3.','The subtraction removes two of the three a3T contributions.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[RES],steps=['First: a2+a3T=R/T².','Second: 2a2+3a3T=S/T.','Second minus twice first: a3T=S/T−2R/T².'])
ask(c,'For θ0=1 rad, θf=5 rad, v0=1 rad/s, vf=0 rad/s and T=2 s, what are R and S?', ['R=2 rad and S=−1 rad/s','R=4 rad and S=−1 rad/s','R=2 rad and S=1 rad/s','R=6 rad and S=0 rad/s'], ['R=5−1−1×2=2; S=0−1=−1.','This uses plain displacement and omits v0T.','The velocity difference is final minus initial.','This adds initial linear motion and omits the initial velocity in S.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[RES],steps=['Subtract initial position and initial linear contribution: R=5−1−2=2 rad.','Subtract endpoint velocities: S=0−1=−1 rad/s.'])
c=concept(489,'General cubic coefficients','Arbitrary endpoint rates, coefficient evaluation and limitations of an illustrative S-curve.')
ask(c,'Which expression is the general cubic coefficient a2?', ['[3(θf−θ0)−(2v0+vf)T]/T²','[3(θf−θ0)−(v0+2vf)T]/T²','[3(θf−θ0)+(2v0+vf)T]/T²','[3(θf−θ0)−(2v0+vf)T]/T³'], ['Elimination gives the double weighting of the initial velocity and a T² denominator.','This interchanges the initial and final velocity weights.','The velocity contribution has the opposite sign.','The denominator gives incorrect coefficient dimensions.'],formula=[GEN])
ask(c,'Which expression is the general cubic coefficient a3?', ['[−2(θf−θ0)+(v0+vf)T]/T³','[2(θf−θ0)+(v0+vf)T]/T³','[−2(θf−θ0)+(v0−vf)T]/T³','[−2(θf−θ0)+(v0+vf)T]/T²'], ['Substitution of the residual definitions produces the sum of endpoint velocities.','The displacement term has the wrong sign.','The endpoint velocities add here.','This has acceleration-coefficient dimensions, not cubic-coefficient dimensions.'],formula=[GEN])
ask(c,'For θ0=1 rad, θf=5 rad, v0=1 rad/s, vf=0 and T=2 s, which cubic is obtained?', ['θ(t)=1+t+2t²−0.75t³','θ(t)=1+t+3t²−t³','θ(t)=1+3t²−t³','θ(t)=1+t−2t²+0.75t³'], ['a2=(12−4)/4=2 and a3=(−8+2)/8=−0.75.','These coefficients fail the supplied nonzero initial-velocity endpoint data.','This sets the initial velocity to zero.','Both nonlinear coefficients have reversed signs.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[GEN,CUB],steps=['a0=1 and a1=1.','a2=[3×4−(2×1+0)×2]/4=2 rad/s².','a3=[−2×4+(1+0)×2]/8=−0.75 rad/s³.','Check θ(2)=1+2+8−6=5 and v(2)=1+8−9=0.'])
ask(c,'Does the positive S-shaped position sketch beside the general coefficient formulas prove that every choice of endpoint velocities gives monotone motion?', ['No; the sketch illustrates one profile, while arbitrary endpoint velocities can cause reversals','Yes; every cubic is monotone on every positive time interval','Yes; specifying endpoints fixes the derivative sign everywhere','No; every cubic must reverse direction'], ['Derivative sign depends on the endpoint data and coefficients.','A quadratic velocity can change sign.','Two positions do not constrain all intermediate derivative signs.','Many rest-to-rest cubics are monotone.'],kind='diagram',difficulty='medium')
persist()
# Slice 3: rest-to-rest profiles, via velocities, quintics and linear limitations.
c=concept(490,'Rest-to-rest cubic shape','Normalized time, boundary checks, monotonicity and the no-displacement special case.')
ask(c,'With D=θf−θ0 and u=t/T, which rest-to-rest cubic is correct?', ['θ=θ0+D(3u²−2u³)','θ=θ0+D(2u²−3u³)','θ=θ0+D(3u−2u²)','θ=θ0+D u'], ['The normalized factor equals 0 and 1 at the endpoints and has zero derivative at both.','At u=1 this gives −D rather than D.','Its derivative is not zero at both endpoints.','A nonzero constant slope does not satisfy rest-to-rest velocities.'],formula=[REST])
ask(c,'For a rest-to-rest cubic with positive displacement D, what does the velocity plot show on 0<t<T?', ['A positive concave-down parabola with zero endpoint velocities','A constant positive velocity with endpoint jumps','A linearly increasing velocity that stays positive at T','Two separate constant-velocity plateaus'], ['v=6D u(1−u)/T is quadratic and positive inside the interval.','That describes an unblended linear position trajectory.','A cubic rest-to-rest velocity returns to zero at T.','The source curve is a parabola, not plateaus.'],kind='diagram',formula=[REST],assumptions=['D>0 and T>0.'])
ask(c,'For the rest-to-rest cubic, what is the position at t=T/2?', ['(θ0+θf)/2','θ0+(θf−θ0)/4','θf','θ0'], ['At u=1/2, 3u²−2u³=1/2.','The normalized factor is 1/2, not 1/4.','The final position is reached at T for nonzero monotone displacement.','The initial position is reached at zero for nonzero displacement.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[REST],steps=['u=1/2 gives 3/4−2/8=1/2.','θ=θ0+(θf−θ0)/2=(θ0+θf)/2.'])
ask(c,'If θf=θ0 and both endpoint velocities are zero, what does the unique cubic give?', ['A constant position with zero velocity and acceleration','A mandatory overshoot followed by a return','A nonzero constant velocity','A singular system for every positive T'], ['D=0 sets both nonlinear coefficients and the velocity to zero.','The endpoint data yield the constant solution, not a mandatory excursion.','Nonzero constant velocity would change position.','The coefficient system remains nonsingular when T>0.'],formula=[REST],assumptions=TIME)
c=concept(491,'Cubic extrema, signs and duration scaling','Peak signed velocity versus speed, acceleration, jerk, limits and boundary smoothness.')
PEAK=fm('max|v|=3|D|/(2T); max|a|=6|D|/T²; j=−12D/T³',{'v':'joint velocity','a':'joint acceleration','j':'joint jerk da/dt inside the segment','D':'signed joint displacement','T':'positive duration'},{'v':'joint-angle unit/s','a':'joint-angle unit/s²','j':'joint-angle unit/s³','D':'joint-angle unit','T':'s'},['Rest-to-rest cubic on 0≤t≤T; jerk formula applies away from any outside hold transition.'])
ask(c,'For D>0, where is the rest-to-rest cubic’s velocity maximum and what is its value?', ['At T/2, with 3D/(2T)','At T, with 6D/T','At T/3, with D/T','At 0, with 3D/T²'], ['Setting acceleration to zero gives T/2; substitution gives 3D/(2T).','The final velocity is zero.','The stationary time and peak value differ.','The initial velocity is zero and D/T² has acceleration dimensions.'],formula=[REST,PEAK],steps=['Set a=6D(1−2t/T)/T²=0.','For nonzero D, t=T/2.','v=6D(1/2)(1/2)/T=3D/(2T).'])
ask(c,'A rest-to-rest move goes from 70° to 10° in 5 s. How should the printed “maximum velocity” expression be interpreted?', ['The midpoint velocity is −18°/s, the signed minimum; maximum speed is 18°/s','The midpoint velocity is +18°/s because maximum always means positive','The signed maximum velocity is −18°/s and endpoint velocity is also negative','The speed is −18°/s'], ['D=−60° gives a negative parabola; its largest magnitude is 18°/s, while signed maximum is zero at the endpoints.','The direction of displacement controls the sign.','Endpoint velocities are zero, greater than −18°/s.','Speed is nonnegative.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[REST,PEAK],steps=['D=10−70=−60°.','v(T/2)=3(−60)/(2×5)=−18°/s.','v is nonpositive on the interval, so −18 is its minimum and 18 is peak speed.'],conflict=(491,'Maximum velocity = 3(θf−θ0)/(2tf).','This is the maximum signed velocity only for positive displacement. For negative displacement it is the minimum signed velocity; the speed maximum uses the displacement magnitude.'))
ask(c,'What are the endpoint accelerations for a rest-to-rest cubic?', ['a(0)=6D/T² and a(T)=−6D/T²','a(0)=a(T)=0','a(0)=a(T)=6D/T²','a(0)=3D/T and a(T)=−3D/T'], ['The linear acceleration changes sign between equal-magnitude endpoints.','Zero endpoint velocity does not force zero endpoint acceleration.','The acceleration slope is nonzero for nonzero D.','These have velocity dimensions, not acceleration dimensions.'],formula=[REST])
ask(c,'Keeping the displacement and normalized shape fixed, doubling the duration changes peak speed and peak acceleration magnitude by which factors?', ['1/2 and 1/4','1/4 and 1/2','2 and 4','1/2 and 1/2'], ['Velocity scales as T⁻¹ and acceleration as T⁻².','These scalings are interchanged.','Longer duration reduces, rather than raises, these magnitudes.','Acceleration has an additional inverse-duration factor.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[PEAK],steps=['Replace T by 2T in 3|D|/(2T): ratio 1/2.','Replace T by 2T in 6|D|/T²: ratio 1/4.'])
ask(c,'What is the jerk inside a rest-to-rest cubic segment?', ['−12D/T³, constant','−12Dt/T³, linear in time','6D/T², constant','Zero for every rest-to-rest displacement'], ['Differentiating the linear acceleration removes t.','This retains a time factor that differentiation removes.','This is the initial acceleration, not jerk.','Nonzero D produces nonzero constant segment jerk.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[REST,PEAK],steps=['a=6D/T²−12Dt/T³.','Differentiate with respect to t: j=−12D/T³.'],assumptions=['Interior of the segment; outside holds may introduce additional discontinuities.'])
ask(c,'A rest-to-rest cubic moves through |D|=60° with speed limit 20°/s and acceleration limit 15°/s². What is the smallest duration satisfying both kinematic bounds?', ['√24 s, approximately 4.899 s','4.5 s','24 s','3 s'], ['Speed requires T≥90/20=4.5 s; acceleration requires T≥√(360/15)=√24 s, which is larger.','This meets the speed condition but gives acceleration 360/20.25≈17.778°/s².','The acceleration condition bounds T² by 24 s², not T by 24 s.','This violates both bounds.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[PEAK,fm('T≥max(3|D|/(2v_lim),√(6|D|/a_lim))',{'D':'signed displacement','T':'duration','v_lim':'positive speed bound','a_lim':'positive acceleration-magnitude bound'},{'D':'deg','T':'s','v_lim':'deg/s','a_lim':'deg/s²'},['Rest-to-rest cubic; only these two kinematic magnitude limits are considered.'])],steps=['Peak speed bound: 3×60/(2T)≤20 implies T≥4.5 s.','Peak acceleration bound: 6×60/T²≤15 implies T²≥24 s².','Choose the larger bound, T=√24≈4.899 s.'],assumptions=['No additional torque, jerk or workspace constraints are imposed; degrees are used consistently.'])
ask(c,'Extend a nonzero rest-to-rest cubic by constant positions before and after its motion interval. What is its continuity at those hold joins?', ['C1 but not C2','C2 but not C3','Discontinuous position','Discontinuous velocity but continuous acceleration'], ['Position and zero velocity match the holds, but the nonzero cubic endpoint accelerations do not match zero hold acceleration.','C2 fails at the acceleration jumps.','Positions match their respective holds.','Velocity matches; acceleration is what jumps.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[REST],assumptions=['D≠0, T>0 and the outside holds have zero velocity and acceleration.'])
issue(491,'signed-peak','The printed maximum-velocity formula omits the sign qualification for negative displacement.','Use the printed expression for D>0; for D<0 identify the signed minimum and use absolute displacement for peak speed. Treat D=0 separately.')
c=concept(492,'Choosing via velocities and matching accelerations','Specified, heuristic and spline-based choices; common rates, Jacobian mapping and concrete C2 coupling.',[492,486,489])
ask(c,'Which set contains the three via-point velocity choices named in the lecture?', ['User-specified velocities, heuristic assignment, acceleration-continuous spline','Random torques, constant joint masses, arbitrary frame rotations','Forward dynamics, inverse dynamics, friction compensation','Only zero velocities at every via point, with no alternatives'], ['These are the three alternatives listed.','These do not supply the listed velocity-assignment methods.','These are dynamic computations, not the listed three choices.','Zero velocities are a possible choice, not the only one.'],pages=[492])
ask(c,'A user specifies a Cartesian via-point velocity. What extra step is needed before using it as a joint cubic boundary velocity?', ['Map it to compatible joint rates through the kinematics/Jacobian','Insert the Cartesian coordinates directly as all joint angular rates','Differentiate the robot’s total mass','Replace every via velocity by zero without considering the request'], ['Joint coefficients require joint rates, so the task-space velocity must be mapped consistently.','Task velocity and joint rates generally differ in dimension and coordinate meaning.','Mass differentiation does not perform the kinematic map.','That would discard the specified velocity.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[492,480],assumptions=['The requested velocity is feasible; singular or redundant mappings require an appropriate solution choice.'])
ask(c,'What additional matching condition distinguishes an acceleration-continuous join from merely assigning one common via velocity?', ['Left and right accelerations agree at the via point','Both segments have the same degree and nothing else','The via-point position is deleted','The final endpoint velocity is ignored'], ['Common position and velocity give C1; matching acceleration supplies C2.','Equal polynomial degree does not force equal derivatives at the join.','The via position remains part of the interpolation problem.','End conditions still matter.'],pages=[492])
ask(c,'Two 1 s cubics interpolate 0→1→2 rad, with zero initial and final velocity and a shared via velocity v. Their join accelerations are −6+4v and 6−4v rad/s² when v is in rad/s. Which v makes the acceleration continuous?', ['1.5 rad/s','0 rad/s','1 rad/s','3 rad/s'], ['Set −6+4v=6−4v, so 8v=12 and v=1.5.','This gives accelerations −6 and +6, a jump.','This gives −2 and +2, still unequal.','This gives +6 and −6, still unequal.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[GEN,CUB],steps=['Use local time 0≤u≤1 s for each segment.','First segment: a2=3−v and a3=−2+v, giving a_left(1)=−6+4v.','Second segment: a2=3−2v and a3=−2+v, giving a_right(0)=6−4v.','Equate the two values and solve v=12/8=1.5 rad/s; both accelerations then equal zero.'],assumptions=['All positions are in radians; the numerical coefficient expressions use seconds and v in rad/s. The joint has no additional speed or effort constraint.'])
c=concept(493,'Quintic boundary control','Six coefficients, derivatives, acceleration endpoints and the zero-rate zero-acceleration normalized profile.')
QUIN=fm('θ=Σ(k=0..5) a_k t^k; v=a1+2a2t+3a3t²+4a4t³+5a5t⁴; a=2a2+6a3t+12a4t²+20a5t³',{'θ':'joint position','v':'joint velocity','a':'joint acceleration','a_k':'coefficient of t^k','k':'power index 0 through 5','t':'local segment time'},{'θ':'joint-angle unit','v':'joint-angle unit/s','a':'joint-angle unit/s²','a_k':'joint-angle unit/s^k','k':'dimensionless','t':'s'},['A fifth-degree or lower scalar polynomial; positive duration.'])
ask(c,'Which endpoint quantities provide the six standard quintic boundary conditions?', ['Position, velocity and acceleration at each of the two endpoints','Position at six arbitrary times and no endpoint derivatives necessarily','Position and velocity at both endpoints, with torque replacing both accelerations','Only acceleration at the initial endpoint repeated six times'], ['The slide prescribes three derivative levels at each endpoint.','This is another interpolation problem, not the listed endpoint specification.','Torque is not a polynomial derivative boundary condition in this construction.','Repeating one condition does not supply six independent constraints.'],formula=[QUIN])
ask(c,'For θ=Σ(k=0..5)a_k t^k, which terms does the quintic add to the cubic acceleration?', ['12a4t²+20a5t³','4a4t³+5a5t⁴','8a4t²+10a5t³','a4t⁴+a5t⁵'], ['Each term is differentiated twice: 4×3 and 5×4.','These are the extra velocity terms.','The second power-rule factors are wrong.','These are the original position terms.'],formula=[QUIN])
ask(c,'For specified θ(0)=θ0, v(0)=v0 and a(0)=α0, which initial coefficients follow?', ['a0=θ0, a1=v0, a2=α0/2','a0=θ0, a1=v0, a2=α0','a0=v0, a1=α0, a2=θ0/2','a0=a1=a2=0 for all endpoint data'], ['At zero, the first three derivative values are a0,a1,2a2.','The acceleration equation contains the factor two.','This mixes position, rate and acceleration coefficients.','Only zero initial data make all three vanish.'],formula=[QUIN],assumptions=['α0 denotes initial joint acceleration, not angular position.'])
QREST=fm('u=t/T; θ=θ0+D(10u³−15u⁴+6u⁵)',{'u':'normalized time','t':'local time','T':'duration','θ':'joint position','θ0':'initial joint position','D':'θf−θ0, signed displacement','θf':'final joint position'},{'u':'dimensionless','t,T':'s','θ,θ0,θf,D':'joint-angle unit'},['T>0; zero velocity and acceleration at both endpoints.'])
ask(c,'Deriving from the six endpoint constraints, which normalized quintic has zero velocity and acceleration at both ends?', ['θ=θ0+D(10u³−15u⁴+6u⁵)','θ=θ0+D(3u²−2u³)','θ=θ0+D(6u³−15u⁴+10u⁵)','θ=θ0+D(10u²−15u³+6u⁴)'], ['The coefficients satisfy endpoint position plus both derivative conditions.','The cubic has nonzero endpoint accelerations for D≠0.','Although its coefficients sum to one, its endpoint derivative conditions fail.','The powers are shifted and the initial second derivative is nonzero.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[QUIN,QREST],steps=['Zero initial velocity and acceleration remove linear and quadratic terms from the displacement polynomial.','Write the normalized factor Au³+Bu⁴+Cu⁵.','At u=1: A+B+C=1, 3A+4B+5C=0, 6A+12B+20C=0.','Solve A=10, B=−15, C=6.'],assumptions=['This is a derived consequence of p493, not an additional printed coefficient formula; D=θf−θ0 and u=t/T.'])
ask(c,'Why can a zero-velocity, zero-acceleration endpoint quintic join constant-position holds with C2 continuity?', ['The position, velocity and acceleration match the corresponding hold values','Every quintic is C2 across any arbitrary join regardless of boundary values','The hold must have nonzero velocity','The quintic has zero displacement for every input'], ['The endpoint constraints supply the three required matching values.','Piecewise continuity depends on matching, not the degree alone.','Constant-position holds have zero velocity.','A nonzero displacement is compatible with zero endpoint derivatives.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[QREST],assumptions=['Each hold equals the adjacent endpoint position and the quintic uses the stated zero endpoint derivatives.'])
ask(c,'Does imposing zero endpoint velocity and acceleration on that quintic also guarantee zero endpoint jerk?', ['No; for D≠0 its endpoint jerk is 60D/T³','Yes; all higher derivatives must then vanish','Yes; degree five forces zero third derivatives at both ends','No; its endpoint jerk must be infinite inside the polynomial'], ['Three differentiations of 10u³−15u⁴+6u⁵ give 60−360u+360u², equal to 60 at both endpoints.','The six constraints do not prescribe third derivatives.','A quintic has a quadratic third derivative, generally nonzero.','A finite polynomial and positive duration give finite derivatives.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[QREST,fm('j=(D/T³)(60−360u+360u²)',{'j':'joint jerk d³θ/dt³','D':'signed joint displacement','T':'positive duration','u':'t/T, normalized time'},{'j':'joint-angle unit/s³','D':'joint-angle unit','T':'s','u':'dimensionless'},['Zero-velocity, zero-acceleration endpoint quintic.'])],steps=['Differentiate its normalized factor three times.','At u=0 or 1 the third derivative is 60.','Thus j=60D/T³ at both endpoints, not zero when D≠0.'])
c=concept(494,'Linear interpolation and interior motion','Two endpoint positions, constant slope, and derivative behavior inside the interval.')
LIN=fm('θ=θ0+Dt/T; v=D/T; a=0 for 0<t<T',{'θ':'joint position','θ0':'initial joint position','D':'θf−θ0, signed displacement','θf':'final joint position','t':'local time','T':'positive duration','v':'joint velocity','a':'joint acceleration'},{'θ,θ0,θf,D':'joint-angle unit','t,T':'s','v':'joint-angle unit/s','a':'joint-angle unit/s²'},['The acceleration statement concerns the open segment, not adjoining stationary holds.'])
ask(c,'For linear interpolation between two positions over positive duration T, what are velocity and acceleration inside the segment?', ['v=(θf−θ0)/T and a=0','v=0 and a=(θf−θ0)/T²','v=2(θf−θ0)t/T² and a=2(θf−θ0)/T²','v=(θf−θ0)T and a=0'], ['The slope is constant, so its time derivative is zero.','Nonzero displacement cannot be produced by zero velocity throughout.','This describes a quadratic position term, not the linear interpolant.','Displacement divided by duration gives velocity, not their product.'],formula=[LIN])
ask(c,'What does the straight position-time line in the p494 diagram encode?', ['Constant joint velocity during the interior of the move','Constant joint position throughout the move','A parabolic velocity with a midpoint maximum','Zero displacement whenever duration is positive'], ['A straight line has a constant slope.','A changing position line is not horizontal.','A parabolic velocity belongs to a cubic position profile.','Its nonzero slope produces displacement.'],kind='diagram',formula=[LIN])
c=concept(495,'Linear endpoint jumps and blending','Piecewise hold-extension continuity, ideal acceleration impulses and smoothing.')
ask(c,'A nonzero linear move is preceded by a stationary hold. At the start, velocity changes from 0 to D/T instantaneously. Which limitation results?', ['The whole piecewise motion is not C1 at the start','The linear polynomial is nondifferentiable at every interior time','The position necessarily jumps by D at the start','The move cannot reach its final position'], ['The one-sided velocities differ, although the positions can match.','The polynomial is smooth in its interior.','Its initial position is θ0, matching the hold.','Endpoint position interpolation still works.'],formula=[LIN],assumptions=['D≠0 and T>0; the holds are part of the trajectory.'])
ask(c,'For a positive-displacement linear move with stationary holds on both sides, what are the signs of the ideal acceleration impulses?', ['Positive at the start and negative at the stop','Positive at both endpoints','Negative at both endpoints','Neither endpoint has an acceleration impulse'], ['Velocity steps up from 0 to D/T, then down to 0.','The stop decreases, rather than increases, velocity.','The start increases velocity.','The ideal velocity steps generate derivative impulses.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['D>0; the endpoint changes are idealized as instantaneous.'])
ask(c,'How should “linear interpolation is only C0” be scoped?', ['It describes the unblended motion joined to stationary holds, not the isolated linear polynomial’s interior','It means a straight line is discontinuous at every point','It means every linear polynomial has a jump in acceleration at every time','It means linear interpolation can never be useful even with smoothing'], ['The piecewise extension has velocity jumps; a single linear polynomial is smooth on its domain.','A linear polynomial is continuous.','Its interior acceleration is constantly zero.','The slide itself recommends blending to address endpoint problems.'],difficulty='medium',kind='exception',conflict=(495,'Linear interpolation is only C0 continuous.','The stated endpoint-velocity jumps arise when a nonzero-slope segment meets stationary holds. The isolated polynomial is smooth; the piecewise extension is C0 but not C1.'))
ask(c,'What remedy is named for abrupt velocity changes in the simple linear scheme?', ['Blend or smooth the transitions rather than switch velocity instantaneously','Make the duration exactly zero','Ignore the start and stop velocities while claiming smoothness','Increase the commanded velocity jump'], ['Blending introduces a transition interval instead of an instantaneous rate change.','Zero duration does not resolve a nonzero-displacement motion.','Ignoring a discontinuity does not eliminate it.','A larger instantaneous jump worsens the ideal acceleration demand.'])
