ask(c,'What are the shapes of t, theta, theta_dot and theta_ddot in the printed program?', ['Each has shape (100,)','t has shape (100,100), and the others are scalars','Each has shape (2,) because two coefficients were solved','Each has shape (3,100) because three plots are created'], ['The polynomial operations preserve the one-dimensional time array’s shape.','linspace returns a one-dimensional array here.','Coefficient count does not set the sample count.','The plotting layout does not reshape the data arrays.'],kind='code_output',steps=['t contains 100 samples.','Scalar-array multiplication and addition broadcast to the same shape for each trajectory quantity.'])
ask(c,'How are theta_dot and theta_ddot calculated in the printed code?', ['By evaluating exact analytical derivative polynomials at the sampled times','By calling np.gradient on sampled theta','By solving a new linear system for each time sample','By taking finite differences of plot pixels'], ['The source explicitly uses a1+2*a2*t+3*a3*t**2 and 2*a2+6*a3*t.','np.gradient is not called.','Only one coefficient solve occurs.','Plot pixels are outputs, not inputs to differentiation.'],kind='code_output',steps=['Read the two explicit derivative expressions.','Their coefficients follow the power rule for the already solved position polynomial.'])
ask(c,'Does the 100-point grid include the analytical velocity peak at t=2.5 s?', ['No; the nearest grid times are 245/99 and 250/99 s','Yes; every even sample count includes its midpoint','Yes; linspace always inserts all polynomial extrema','No; the grid excludes both endpoints as well'], ['A midpoint sample would require index 49.5 among integer indices 0 through 99.','An even count with both endpoints has two middle samples straddling the midpoint.','linspace uses equal spacing, not extrema detection.','Both endpoints are included.'],kind='code_output',difficulty='medium',steps=['The samples are t_k=5k/99 for integer k=0,…,99.','Solving 5k/99=2.5 gives k=49.5, not an integer.','The adjacent indices are 49 and 50.'])
ask(c,'Ignoring floating-point roundoff, what is the maximum sampled velocity on that 100-point grid, compared with the analytical peak 18°/s?', ['18(1−1/99²)°/s, approximately 17.998163°/s','Exactly 18°/s, because the time grid includes 2.5 s','18(1+1/99²)°/s, slightly above the analytical maximum','Exactly 17°/s, because values are rounded to integers'], ['The nearest normalized times are 1/2±1/198; substituting in 72u(1−u) gives 18(1−1/99²).','The midpoint is not sampled.','A sample of the same curve cannot exceed its analytical maximum.','The arrays retain floating-point values rather than rounding to integers.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='hard',pages=[499,498],formula=[REST],steps=['For D=60° and T=5 s, v=72u(1−u)°/s.','Nearest grid values are u=49/99 and 50/99, each 1/198 from 1/2.','u(1−u)=1/4−1/198².','Thus v=18−72/198²=18(1−1/99²)≈17.998163°/s.'],assumptions=['Use ideal arithmetic for the comparison; the two theoretical maxima can differ by roundoff in a NumPy run.'])
